{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-5-20250929","id":"msg_01QTnxrfnrqRiWVJDCDjijih","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I can see the exact problem. The canonical name is \"Wilson\" (the bare last name), and it has aliases including both \"George B. Wilson\", \"George Wilson\" AND \"Myrtle\", \"Myrtle Wilson\". This is the catastrophic merge.\n\nThe root cause is that the LLM or the validation logic is failing to recognize that when you have:\n- Different FIRST names (George vs. Myrtle)\n- Same LAST name (Wilson)  \n- Gendered titles or implications\n\nThese should NEVER be merged. The issue is in the `_validate_merge` function. Let me look more carefully at lines 1634-1640:\n\n```python\n# If they have different first names and no birth name evidence, reject\nif first1 != first2 and first1 and first2:\n    logger.debug(\n        f\"Merge rejected: {canonical} <-> {alias} \"\n        f\"(different first names, same last name - likely family members)\"\n    )\n    return False, 0.1\n```\n\nThis check exists! But it's nested within a specific condition that only triggers when `shared_might_be_lastname` is True and `len(names_with_lastname) > 1`. The problem is that this check might not be running in all cases.\n\nLet me trace through the logic more carefully. The fix needs to be earlier and more aggressive. When comparing ANY two names that:\n1. Share only a last name (no shared first name)\n2. Both have DIFFERENT first names\n\nThey should be rejected IMMEDIATELY, before any complex validation.\n\nLet me implement the fix:"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":5,"cache_creation_input_tokens":465,"cache_read_input_tokens":72080,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":465,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5e1dd3f8-a824-44bb-9974-11f838400830","uuid":"f663190a-967a-4